const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Scientific Medical Communication","serviceType":"Medical communication services","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/scientific-communication/","description":"Six kinds of medical output, six different rulebooks. The map between them — ICMJE and COPE for papers, ICH and MDR for submissions.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What is scientific medical communication, and what does this page actually cover?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It is everything an organisation publishes about medicine, and the useful definition is negative: it is not one discipline with one rulebook. A journal article answers to the ICMJE and COPE. A submission document answers to an ICH guideline or an MDR annex. An accredited education activity answers to the ACCME Standards or EACCME criteria. Promotional material answers to the ABPI Code, EFPIA, FDA rules, PAAB, the Therapeutic Goods Advertising Code or the PMD Act, by market. A corporate white paper usually answers to nothing formal at all. This page is the map between outputs and frameworks, and the six services underneath it each handle one part of that: scientific and academic writing, regulatory writing, CME content development, marketing communication, thought leadership and editorial design and editorial and quality support."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do COPE and the ICMJE apply to our marketing material?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. They govern journal publication: authorship of research papers, editorial decisions, corrections, retractions, publication ethics. They say nothing about a brochure, a website page, a sales aid, a campaign or an executive report, and citing them on a commercial page is not a higher standard — it is a signal that the compliance section was copied from an academic page. What governs promotional material is the code of every market it reaches. One genuine crossover is worth knowing: a journal article is not promotional material, but a reprint of it deployed as a sales aid is — the use decides, not the format, and the accompaniments the market’s code requires attach to it. Similarly, GPP 2022 applies to publications arising from company-sponsored research, which is where publication ethics and commercial interests genuinely do meet."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can one supplier write all of it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"One supplier can coordinate all of it. One writer cannot write all of it well, and a supplier offering that is offering you the weakest of the four. A regulatory writer who has produced clinical study reports, a publications writer who has been through journal review, an education writer who understands what an accreditation survey asks for, and a promotional writer who knows the market’s code are different people with different habits — and each of those disciplines punishes the instincts of the others. What genuinely benefits from being coordinated is the layer above: the output map, the sequence constraints, the single disclosure position, and the cross-set check that stops two documents disagreeing about the same number. That is what this page sells; the writing itself is specialised and priced on the child pages."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What order should our outputs go in?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Sequence is a set of constraints rather than a calendar, and there are four that recur. Prior publication: journals differ on what counts, so publishing a finding in a white paper, a press release or on a corporate site can put the paper at risk — check each candidate journal’s policy before, not after. Labelling: promotional material cannot precede the labelling it describes, so it is drafted against proposed text, held, and finalised against approved text with the version recorded. Product independence: accredited education cannot be built round a product, which usually means it has no dependency on the commercial timeline at all and can move earlier. Statutory deadlines: a lay summary of trial results under EU CTR Article 37(4) is due within twelve months of the end of the trial, or six with paediatric participants — a legal obligation that routinely appears on no team’s plan because it belongs to none of them."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do you keep guideline versions current?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"By checking at the start of each project rather than carrying a template forward, and by saying on the page when we could not check something. In the last two years: CONSORT 2025 and SPIRIT 2025 replaced their predecessors in April 2025, both stating the old versions should no longer be used. The ICMJE Recommendations were updated in January 2026 — and icmje.org’s HTML pages still showed the 2024 text when we checked, so the PDF is the one to cite. ISO 14155 and ISO 20417 were both replaced in March 2026. ICH E6 moved to R3 and E2D to R1. The ACCME Standards for Integrity and Independence replaced the Standards for Commercial Support with effect from 1 January 2022. The ABPI Code is now in its 2024 edition. A template carried forward misses every one of those, and it is not carelessness — it is the default behaviour of a competent team working to a deadline, which is precisely why the check has to be a step rather than an intention."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How is confidentiality maintained across a programme?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A non-disclosure agreement is signed before any material moves, and writers and project staff are bound by internal confidentiality agreements as well. Unpublished data, submission drafts, labelling texts, agency correspondence and launch plans are held under the UK GDPR on access-controlled systems within a quality management system certified to ISO 9001:2015, certificate H3CR9TCU20251212INDQM10N, kept only as long as the project needs them and never used to train anything. Two things specific to programmes of this kind. Access is per output, not per client — the writer on the promotional material does not need the submission dossier, and does not get it. And on conflicts: we do not take competing products in the same indication concurrently, and where a potential conflict exists we tell you before accepting the work rather than managing it quietly."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What sets your service apart?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The honest answer is narrower than the question invites. A page that answers it with the word “Best” in its own title is making a claim that means nothing in a regulated market and carries a comparative-advertising risk in several of them. What we will say is this. Every framework on this page is named with its version and the date we checked it. Where we could not verify something, the page says so instead of filling the gap — there are several such gaps stated on these pages, and stating them is deliberate. Every price has its scope cap printed beside it. Every sample is a complete deliverable published on the page rather than a teaser behind a form, including the parts where the finding went against the client. And where a claim commonly made in this category is wrong, the page says so and shows you how to check it, because how a supplier handles a checkable claim is the most informative thing you can observe before hiring them."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Scientific communication","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/scientific-communication/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Services","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/scientific-communication/"}]}];

